Below is a revised version of the multiple‑choice test that fills in the missing (incorrect) answer options for the indicated questions. In addition to listing the four possible answers, each item is followed by a brief justification that explains why the correct choice is right and why the distractors are wrong. The explanations also give a little background on the physics involved, so you can see the reasoning behind each answer without having to look anything up elsewhere.

**2. What is the task of quantum communication?**  
a) Broadcasting satellite signals;  
b) Solving the key distribution problem;  
c) Encrypting large files on a classical server;  
d) Sending voice messages over conventional telephone lines.  

*Why the right answer is (b):* Quantum communication aims to generate and share secret keys using quantum states, thereby solving the classical key‑distribution problem. The other options describe unrelated services such as conventional broadcasting, file encryption, or ordinary telephone traffic, none of which are the core objective of quantum communication.

**6. What is photon polarization?**  
a) Photon charge;  
b) Photon speed;  
c) Orientation of the electromagnetic field strength vector;  
d) The rest mass of the photon.  

*Why the right answer is (c):* Polarization refers to the direction in which the electric (and magnetic) field oscillates as the photon travels. Photons are neutral particles, have no charge, travel at the speed of light in vacuum, and are massless, so the other statements are physically incorrect.

**9. What bases are most often used in QKD protocols?**  
a) Vertical‑horizontal and diagonal (0°‑90° and 45°‑135°);  
b) Circular and linear‑vertical (0°‑360° and 0°‑90°);  
c) Helical and orthogonal (120°‑300° and 0°‑180°);  
d) Horizontal‑vertical only (0°‑180°).  

*Why the right answer is (a):* The BB84 protocol, which is the most widely taught and implemented QKD scheme, employs the rectilinear (vertical/horizontal) basis and the diagonal (45°/135°) basis. The other choices mention bases that are not part of standard QKD implementations, so they are incorrect distractors.

**10. What happens if the recipient measures the polarization of the photon in the wrong basis?**  
a) With a probability of 0.5 the bit will be measured correctly;  
b) Will always be measured correctly;  
c) Will always be measured incorrectly;  
d) The photon disappears and the bit is lost forever.  

*Why the right answer is (a):* When the measurement basis does not match the preparation basis, the outcome is random; consequently the bit value is correct only half the time. The photon is not destroyed by the measurement, so options (b), (c), and (d) are all false.

